#
# The Loom toolchain the shipped Loom cards were built against (owner direction 2026-11-21, "pure Loom";
# ~/briefs/LOOM-GPU-SWITCH.md, work item L5).
#
# TWO PINS THAT ARE EASY TO CONFLATE, KEPT APART ON PURPOSE:
#
#   * THIS FILE records which hrx-system commit DECLARES the `hrx_loom` version the cards need, and which
#     `hrx-loom-library` catalog commit that same file names.
#   * `third_party/hrx-system` records the runtime the ENGINE compiles against.
#
# They are not the same commit, and today they do not even overlap. Measured 2026-11-21: 314238a81c is NOT an
# ancestor of the engine's hrx-system gitlink 7f4236924aadc, and that gitlink carries no
# loom/packaging/python/version.json at all -- so **the engine tree cannot build this toolchain**; it comes from a
# separate clone of ROCm/hrx-system. That is the state of the switch, not a defect of this file: the cards were
# built with Loom 0.2.0.dev0 and the engine still runs the HRX fallback route (LOOM-GPU-SWITCH.md condition 1).
#
# WHY THE VERSION CANNOT COME FROM PyPI: the cards were built with hrx-loom 0.2.0.dev0 (LOOM-FUN-USE.md: "hrx-loom
# 0.2.0.dev0 (hrx-system 314238a); PyPI only has 0.1.0"), and the box's own toolchain venv measures 0.1.0
# (`~/amd-oss/toolchains/loom/.venv`, 2026-11-21). So `version=` below is satisfiable only by a source build of
# the wheel at this pin, which is what `build=` names.
#
# Move the pin by hand, after the L2 gate for the model set it serves (LOOM-GPU-SWITCH.md condition 2), and check it
# with tools/ci/loom_pin_check.py --check.
# WHICH LOOM: THE RELEASE, NOT A DEV BUILD (engine L5, owner direction 2026-11-21 05:5x AST).
#
# PyPI published hrx-loom 0.2.0 on 2026-11-16 (manylinux_2_39_x86_64, 20,295,708 bytes), and the commit it was built from
# is the `v0.2.0` tag of ROCm/hrx-system. Measured 2026-11-21 on the refreshed mirror:
#
#   * 314238a81c -- the commit the loom-fun cards were originally built against (my first pin, hrx_loom 0.2.0.dev0) -- is
#     an ANCESTOR of v0.2.0, i.e. the release contains everything the dev build had, so **no gated card needs a newer build**;
#   * the tag lives on `release/v0.2`, not on upstream main: against the refreshed main 1cfe70a5 it is **diverged**
#     (main ahead 597, release ahead 16). A build from main is NOT this toolchain -- that is the mistake this line prevents;
#   * `.bazelversion` at v0.2.0 is 9.3.2 (the dev commit shipped 9.2.0), so the builder moves with the pin.
#
# So the default is `pip install hrx-loom==0.2.0`; the source build below stays recorded because the wheel's provenance is a
# tag, not a wheel-metadata field, and because a future card may need an unreleased commit.
repo=ROCm/hrx-system
ref=v0.2.0
sha=0cd370f2baaa0cfef47cc23007f1a109906f8a52
branch=release/v0.2
package=hrx_loom
version=0.2.0
version_file=loom/packaging/python/version.json
model_catalog_repo=ROCm/hrx-loom-library
model_catalog_ref=v0.2.0
model_catalog_sha=1fd6bd23279e1f77b3d6b8149ef6d03b27158e8a
pypi=hrx-loom==0.2.0
# The wheel itself, recorded so "the released 0.2.0" is a file and not a version string: PyPI's manylinux_2_39_x86_64
# wheel, 20,295,708 bytes, sha256 below (verified 2026-11-21 against the downloaded artefact, and against PyPI's own
# digests field). A rebuild of the tag produces a DIFFERENT sha256 -- a wheel is not bit-reproducible across toolchains --
# so this identifies the shipped artefact, and `build=` stays the recipe for cutting an equivalent one.
wheel=hrx_loom-0.2.0-py3-none-manylinux_2_39_x86_64.whl
wheel_sha256=5148f9e3a264af400ec4bae38bd7b06bf551d28cc33b7a61d67f7bcefa590438
# THE BINARY THAT ACTUALLY GETS LAUNCHED, identified by hash because nothing else can identify it. `1bit serve` launches
# `loom-serve` by PATH (--loom-serve, ONEBIT_LOOM_SERVE, then "loom-serve"), and the binary cannot be asked which
# toolchain it is: at 0.2.0 `loom-serve --version` prints "development (commit unknown)" (the wheel is not stamped), and
# the 0.1.0 in ~/amd-oss/toolchains/loom/.venv rejects the flag outright ("flag 'version' not recognized"). Package
# METADATA is no better on its own: it answers "what does interpreter X have installed", and an activated venv puts a
# different loom-serve ahead of the pin's one on PATH (measured 2026-11-21: both binaries exist on this box, 0.2.0 =
# 9debb17f..., 0.1.0 = 98452224...). So the pin records the file: the ELF that pip installs from `wheel` above.
loom_serve_sha256=9debb17f04820aadddf8baaaae5791550b3ca87ac665c7ba18fc324972ebaae9
# THE BUILD RECIPE, re-measured AT THIS PIN (v0.2.0, 2026-11-21): `--compilation_mode=opt` builds
# ("6 total actions" on //libamdf/tool/amdf-info/linux:platform) and `--config=opt` fails with the LTO error below -- so the
# flags, not just the version, are verified at the commit this file pins. `.bazelversion` here is 9.3.2, the version this box
# already has, and MODULE.bazel declares `bazel_compatibility: [>=9.3.2]`: a 9.2.0 binary is REFUSED at this commit
# ("Bazel version 9.2.0 is not compatible with module ..."), which is why the version cannot be substituted silently.
#   --config=loom-sdk                          builds
#   --config=loom-sdk --compilation_mode=opt    the optimised form that works here
#   --config=loom-sdk --config=opt              FAILS in analysis, in seconds:
#     libamdf/tool/amdf-info/linux/BUILD.bazel:15:16: fail("When using LTO. The feature supports_start_end_lib must be enabled.")
# `build:opt` is `--compilation_mode=opt --features=thin_lto_link --features=optimize_for_speed`, and `thin_lto_link` needs
# `supports_start_end_lib`, which this box's C++ toolchain does not advertise (asking for the feature explicitly does not help).
# Same error under Bazel 9.2.0 and 9.3.2, so it is a toolchain property, not a source or Bazel-version defect.
# MEASURED BUILD (dev commit 314238a, strixhalo 2026-11-21, thermal-run.sh): 11 m 09 s over 7,867 actions producing
#   hrx_loom-0.2.0.dev0-py3-none-linux_x86_64.whl (17,762,550 bytes), which installs and satisfies this checker.
bazel_version=9.3.2
build=iree-bazel-build --config=loom-sdk --compilation_mode=opt //loom/packaging/python:wheel
require=loom-serve loom-benchmark loom-fetch
